# Infra: how the agent reaches the product's deployed environments

Platform-level guidance for an agent acting on infrastructure once it is deployed. The
image contract, boot entrypoint and environment variables are documented in
[`../dev/deploy.md`](../dev/deploy.md) — read that before touching build, entrypoint or
migrations. The local environment uses none of this.

## Operating rules

**Check the session before concluding something is broken.** An expired credential and a
real failure produce similar symptoms. Confirm the credential is valid before diagnosing.

**Production is read-only by default.** Queries, counts and logs are free. Writing to the
production database, redeploying, changing a variable or altering a firewall requires
explicit confirmation from the user — production holds real customer data.

**Transient failure is common; don't conclude on the first attempt.** Talking to a
provider's API or opening a remote session can fail intermittently. Retry 3–5 times with a
pause before treating it as a problem. A `curl` returning empty is usually a gateway error,
not a refused operation — inspect the raw response body before re-running (it may have
succeeded on the server with only the response lost, which produces a duplicate if you
blindly repeat).

**List before you create.** The user may have created the resource through a console or UI
while you were working. Check current state before any creation.

## Your provider

Product-owned: the template ships no cloud account, VM, panel, DNS/CDN or mail-sending
credential — those are this product's own choice. Fill this section in with how an agent
reaches your actual environments (cloud/VM access, the deploy orchestrator's panel or API,
the database connection path per environment, DNS/CDN, outbound mail), one subsection per
service, in the same shape as the rules above: how to check a session, what is read-only by
default, what always needs the user's explicit confirmation.

The variable names any provider needs to run the images are in `apps/api/.env.example` —
point at that file instead of repeating the list here.
